diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index fb1d80c4..b0225eb8 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -17,11 +17,11 @@ Bring your own API keys — Gemini, and optionally GPT, Grok, or Claude — or r
 
 ## Features
 
-- **Dictate**: Record speech; the transcription inserts at the cursor when auto-paste is on, and is always copied to the clipboard. Use Gemini, OpenAI, or any audio-capable model on OpenRouter, your own OpenAI-compatible server (LiteLLM, Azure, …), or an on-device model offline. Offline, **Parakeet Ultra** is the recommended model: 25 European languages, German and English included, transcribed in a fraction of a second after Stop (about 0.1 s for a sentence, under a second for a minute without Glossary terms) at the accuracy of Whisper Large v3 Turbo, ~700 MB download. On-device models write the German layout commands "Doppelpunkt", "neuer Absatz" and "neue Zeile" as ":", a blank line and a line break; Whisper models stay available for other languages. Temperature and thinking effort are configurable for cloud models. Longer recordings start transcribing while you still speak — Gemini, OpenAI, Grok, and on-device Whisper — so pressing Stop only waits for the last chunk (Parakeet transcribes the whole recording after Stop, which is faster still). If a chunk fails, the app falls back to transcribing the full recording.
+- **Dictate**: Record speech; the transcription inserts at the cursor when auto-paste is on, and is always copied to the clipboard. Use Gemini, OpenAI, or any audio-capable model on OpenRouter, your own OpenAI-compatible server (LiteLLM, Azure, …), or an on-device model offline. Offline, **Parakeet Ultra** is the recommended model: 25 European languages, German and English included, transcribed in a fraction of a second after Stop (about 0.1 s for a sentence, under a second for a minute without Glossary terms) at the accuracy of Whisper Large v3 Turbo, ~700 MB download. On-device models write the German layout commands "Doppelpunkt", "neuer Absatz" and "neue Zeile" as ":", a blank line and a line break, and the Punctuation setting "Only when spoken" (Settings → Dictate) drops the model's own punctuation so only spoken "Punkt", "Komma" (between digits a decimal comma: "3 Komma 5" is "3,5"), "Fragezeichen" and "Ausrufezeichen" are written, plus "Schrägstrich" as "/" (spoken-only); Whisper models stay available for other languages. Temperature and thinking effort are configurable for cloud models. Longer recordings start transcribing while you still speak — Gemini, OpenAI, Grok, and on-device Whisper — so pressing Stop only waits for the last chunk (Parakeet transcribes the whole recording after Stop, which is faster still). If a chunk fails, the app falls back to transcribing the full recording.
 - **Add Selection to Glossary**: Select a correctly spelled term anywhere — your practice software, a document, an email — and use the menu item or a shortcut you set in Settings → General (it has no default shortcut; direct-download build only). It is appended to the Glossary verbatim, with a duplicate check. No model is involved, which makes it the way to grow the Glossary while Offline Mode is on, where the model-driven learning paths (Smart Improvement, the typed-text learner) do not run.
 - **Offline Mode**: One switch (Settings → Privacy & Permissions, also offered during onboarding) that makes the app device-local: dictation runs on an on-device model (Parakeet Ultra or Whisper), requests the app itself builds are blocked at the network layer, and no transcript, prompt or audio sample is written to the usage log. Requests to your own machine or local network still work, so a Whisper server or Ollama on your network stays available. Downloading a Parakeet, Whisper or MLX model from Hugging Face still works (FluidAudio and WhisperKit use their own URLSessions, which the Offline Mode guard does not wrap — that download carries no content of yours; once downloaded, Parakeet loads with FluidAudio's own network switch shut and never re-downloads on its own; a failed load is reported, and Delete + Download in Settings is the repair). Dictate Prompt keeps working through an in-process MLX model (Qwen3 4B Instruct, downloads itself, no server needed) or a local Ollama / LM Studio server if you prefer to run your own. Offline MLX and HTTP-local models read plain text, so they take the text to edit from your clipboard — in the App Store build, copy the text yourself (⌘C) before you start dictating, since that build cannot copy the selection for you. Dictate Prompt also sends no earlier turns as history while it is on, so a note from the previous patient never travels with the next request. It also decodes greedily (temperature 0), so the same dictation is structured the same way every time, and checks every reply word by word against your dictation: words in the result that you did not dictate (headings, punctuation, segment formatting like L4/L5 and simple inflections aside) are listed in a "Not in your dictation" warning — shown even with popups off — while the text itself is pasted unchanged. Chat can run on the same offline MLX models; Read Aloud uses on-device macOS voices. Smart Improvement and the Google and Trello integrations have no on-device equivalent and stop working while it is on. Built for regulated dictation — patient findings, case notes — where "the recording never leaves this device" has to hold whatever else is configured.
 - **Anonymous usage statistics (opt-in, off by default)**: A switch in onboarding and Settings → Privacy & Permissions that, when you turn it on, sends a daily summary of counts to the developer — how often features were used and whether they worked, failure classes, which built-in models ran, which features were used (for example screenshots, chat integrations, AI-app requests), and which features are switched on. Never transcripts, prompts, replies, audio, the apps you paste into, or any identifier. "See exactly what is sent" shows the exact JSON; turning it off deletes anything queued. Unavailable in Offline Mode. The receiving server's code is in `server/telemetry/`.
-- **Auto-paste**: Insert the result at the cursor via simulated ⌘V (Settings → Clipboard Behavior). Off by default; needs the Accessibility permission; direct-download only — the App Store build copies to the clipboard instead. Turn on **Restore clipboard** alongside it for a non-destructive paste: whatever you had copied before dictating goes back on the clipboard right after the text is pasted.
+- **Auto-paste**: Insert the result at the cursor via simulated ⌘V (Settings → Clipboard Behavior). Off by default; needs the Accessibility permission; direct-download only — the App Store build copies to the clipboard instead. Turn on **Restore clipboard** alongside it for a non-destructive paste: whatever you had copied before dictating goes back on the clipboard right after the text is pasted. Or turn on **Clear clipboard after auto-paste** (needs Restore clipboard off) to empty the clipboard right after the paste, so a later ⌘V cannot insert an earlier dictation; Copy Last Transcription still works.
 - **Copy Last Transcription**: Every dictation result stays available in the menu bar — one entry re-copies the most recent transcription, and a **Recent Transcriptions** submenu holds the last five. Use it when an auto-paste landed in the wrong window, or when a later copy overwrote the clipboard.
 - **Dictate Prompt**: Speak an instruction that edits the selected text, for example "make this shorter" or "translate this to English". With nothing selected, it writes what you dictate or ask for instead (for example "write a reply saying I can come on Friday", or a note shaped by your Dictate Prompt system prompt; for a recurring format, use **Template** below) and inserts it at the cursor, without earlier turns from the conversation. Supports Gemini and OpenAI audio-input models, in-process offline MLX models (no server), a local Ollama / LM Studio server, or your own OpenAI-compatible server (the same one Dictate and Chat use); optional screenshots can be included with the prompt. With Gemini and a connected Google account, Trello or shared folder, it can look things up while it writes (read-only: your calendar, tasks, email, Trello cards, shared files), for example "reply and offer my free slot on Thursday"; switch this off in Settings → Dictate Prompt. While recording, the pill grows into a card: its header shows what the run will edit (the selected text, or a warning that nothing is selected and it will write new text), then your quick prompts — Control-1 to Control-9, or ↑/↓ and Return, runs one without speaking. By default these are the instructions you repeat most; in Settings → Dictate Prompt → Quick Prompts you can define your own list (up to nine, in your order) and choose whether your most-used instructions still appear below it. Type your own instruction into the field under the list and press the shortcut again (or Return) to run it. Return on an empty field does the same as ✓: it stops and uses what you said.
 - **Template**: Dictate into a fixed format you set up once. Example: a note with Anamnese, Befund and Therapie. Dictate writes down what you say, Dictate Prompt follows the instruction you speak, Template puts what you say into your format. Set it up in Settings → Templates and give it a key (unassigned by default): press it, dictate, press it again, and the text comes back in that format, in any app, including practice software in a browser. With several templates the card lists them numbered, starting on the last one used: press 1–9, or ↑/↓ and Return. A template can also get a key of its own, for a dictation microphone's buttons. With text selected, the template formats the selection instead, plus anything you say; pressing the key twice without speaking is enough. A template run never uses your Dictate Prompt instructions, writing style, earlier turns or look-up tools, so emails stay emails and one patient's note never reaches the next request. The Dictate Prompt key never applies a template, and a template key does nothing while a Dictate Prompt recording runs. Tab while recording (or a click on the card's header) runs that one recording as plain Dictate Prompt. Offline Mode safeguards apply unchanged (temperature 0, the "Not in your dictation" check, which accepts the template's own headings). Adding templates changes nothing for an existing custom Dictate Prompt system prompt. Got the format when you wanted plain text? **Run Again Without Template** in the menu bar reruns your last template dictation without it for ten minutes (select the formatted text first to replace it); that recording stays in WhisperShortcut's own data folder on this Mac and is deleted after ten minutes, on quit, or at the next launch after a crash.
